          "rules": [
            {
              "id": "vuln-0001",
              "name": "Shell command injection via unescaped `task` interpolation in coding_workflow.py",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Shell command injection via unescaped `task` interpolation in coding_workflow.py"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "The `task` parameter of `coding_workflow()` is interpolated unescaped into four `/bin/bash -c \"...\"` command strings (branch name, `git checkout -b`, `git commit -m`, `git push`). A task string containing a double quote breaks out of the quoted command, allowing arbitrary shell command execution on the AgentCore runtime with the session's privileges. In the repository as committed, `task` comes only from the operator's `sys.argv[1]`; the vulnerability is realized when `task` is supplied by an untrusted source (ticket title, webhook payload, LLM-derived task string), which the function's design (a JIRA-style ticket identifier) makes the plausible integration."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "The `task` parameter of `coding_workflow()` is interpolated unescaped into four `/bin/bash -c \"...\"` command strings (branch name, `git checkout -b`, `git commit -m`, `git push`). A task string containing a double quote breaks out of the quoted command, allowing arbitrary shell command execution on the AgentCore runtime with the session's privileges. In the repository as committed, `task` comes only from the operator's `sys.argv[1]`; the vulnerability is realized when `task` is supplied by an untrusted source (ticket title, webhook payload, LLM-derived task string), which the function's design (a JIRA-style ticket identifier) makes the plausible integration.\n\nArbitrary command execution on the AgentCore runtime container (full read/write/execute within that session's privileges, including the `/workspace` checkout it operates on and any credentials available to the runtime session) whenever a task string under attacker influence reaches the workflow. Demonstrated harmlessly with a file-creation marker; no AWS APIs or deployed targets were contacted.\n\nTreat `task` as untrusted data at the point where it crosses into command construction. Preferred: stop building shell strings entirely — pass commands as argument lists (or have the runtime accept `argv`) so no shell metacharacter interpretation occurs, and derive the branch name and commit message from a validated identifier rather than the raw string. At minimum: validate `task` against a strict allowlist (for example `^[A-Z][A-Z0-9]*-[0-9]+$` for ticket-style identifiers) and reject anything else before it is interpolated; apply `shlex.quote()` to every interpolated value if free-form text must be supported. Both the allowlist and the quoting were verified in a mocked reproduction to block the payload while leaving benign task identifiers working. If ticket content is ever forwarded to `shell.invoke_agent` as well, sanitize or bound it there too to avoid pairing this with prompt injection.",
                "markdown": "The `task` parameter of `coding_workflow()` is interpolated unescaped into four `/bin/bash -c \"...\"` command strings (branch name, `git checkout -b`, `git commit -m`, `git push`). A task string containing a double quote breaks out of the quoted command, allowing arbitrary shell command execution on the AgentCore runtime with the session's privileges. In the repository as committed, `task` comes only from the operator's `sys.argv[1]`; the vulnerability is realized when `task` is supplied by an untrusted source (ticket title, webhook payload, LLM-derived task string), which the function's design (a JIRA-style ticket identifier) makes the plausible integration.\n\nArbitrary command execution on the AgentCore runtime container (full read/write/execute within that session's privileges, including the `/workspace` checkout it operates on and any credentials available to the runtime session) whenever a task string under attacker influence reaches the workflow. Demonstrated harmlessly with a file-creation marker; no AWS APIs or deployed targets were contacted.\n\nTreat `task` as untrusted data at the point where it crosses into command construction. Preferred: stop building shell strings entirely — pass commands as argument lists (or have the runtime accept `argv`) so no shell metacharacter interpretation occurs, and derive the branch name and commit message from a validated identifier rather than the raw string. At minimum: validate `task` against a strict allowlist (for example `^[A-Z][A-Z0-9]*-[0-9]+$` for ticket-style identifiers) and reject anything else before it is interpolated; apply `shlex.quote()` to every interpolated value if free-form text must be supported. Both the allowlist and the quoting were verified in a mocked reproduction to block the payload while leaving benign task identifiers working. If ticket content is ever forwarded to `shell.invoke_agent` as well, sanitize or bound it there too to avoid pairing this with prompt injection."
              },
              "properties": {
                "security-severity": "7.8",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "stride:T",
                  "stride:I"
                ]
              }
            },
